.TH DOTTORE 1 "2026" "Il Dottore" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dottore \- spec-driven security scanner for LLMs and AI applications (nmap-for-AI)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dottore
.I run
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fITARGET\fR...] \fB--scope\fR \fIscope.yaml\fR
.br
.B dottore
.I fingerprint
\fITARGET\fR \fB--scope\fR \fIscope.yaml\fR
.br
.B dottore
.I fleet
\fIfleet.yaml\fR [\fB--run\fR] [\fB--judge\fR \fIjudge.yaml\fR] [\fIrun's store, spec
path and report options\fR]
.br
.B dottore
.I "lint | describe | registry | coverage | new-spec | replay | diff | calibrate | render-media | schema"
[\fIARGS\fR]
.PP
.B dottore --version
prints the package version and the version of the rules that judge a reply, which a resume is
bound to:
.BR "dottore 0.1.0 (judging rules 2)" .
.PP
There is no default command: always type the subcommand.
.PP
.B dottore -v
.I command
(a root option, also
.BR "run -v" )
prints the traceback of an internal error.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dottore
(alias
.BR dott )
examines an AI endpoint, diagnoses its security weaknesses against a declarative attack
battery mapped to the OWASP LLM Top 10, MITRE ATLAS and the Nova IoPC taxonomy, and by
falsification to the OWASP AISVS controls it can catch failing, and issues a reproducible,
evidence-backed report mapped to operational risk. Every spec also carries a NIST AI RMF 1.0
subcategory token (NIST AI 600-1 is a reference, not a mapping the code checks).
.PP
It is a defensive tool. It scans nothing without a
.I scope.yaml
authorization record (endpoint allowlist, default-deny), never performs real destructive actions or
exfiltration (mocked tools and planted canaries), and redacts secrets and PII at rest.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Run a campaign against one or more targets. It is not a default command:
.B dottore target.yaml --scope scope.yaml
is a usage error (exit 3).
.TP
.B fingerprint
Identify the model and guardrails behind an endpoint; attack nothing.
.TP
.B fleet
Expand a fleet.yaml into an authorization scope plus one target file per model; with
.B --run
scan them all. Each http or https scope entry is pinned to its host and port. The judge is declared
in the fleet file's
.B judge:
block, which the scope authorizes and
.B fleet
writes to judge.yaml; a
.B --judge
file must name the same id, endpoint and credential, or it is refused.
.BR --spec-path ,
.BR --evidence-root ,
.B --run-db
and the report flags mean what they mean to
.BR run ;
without
.B --run
they are carried into the dottore run line fleet prints. A ws:// or wss://
endpoint, or provider websocket, is refused before anything is written (exit 3): a fleet entry
carries no websocket block, so that target file is written by hand (OD-37). A WebSocket judge
block is accepted only with a matching hand-written
.B --judge
file, and no judge.yaml is written for it.
.TP
.B lint
Schema, policy and fixtures-prove-detection lint of the specs. A directory with a pack.yaml is
read as a pack (attacks/ and suites/); one without is read as loose specs, its own *.yaml and
*.yml files only. A path that does not exist (PATH_NOT_FOUND) or yields no spec and no suite
file (NO_SPECS, which names the YAML files in its subdirectories it did not read) exits 3; YAML
files a loose tree left unread are a FILES_NOT_READ warning. Exit 0 clean, 1 an error in a spec,
suite or pack, 3 a path that gives nothing to lint (3 wins over 1).
.TP
.B describe
Show one spec's detail card.
.TP
.B registry ls
List the attack-spec catalogue (filter by --category, --owasp, --tag, --suite). A --suite that is
not registered, and a --spec-path that does not exist or yields nothing, are refused (exit 3).
.TP
.B new-spec
Scaffold a new attack spec and empty fixtures. A --family holding a control or format character,
shaped like a key (a credential format or a high-entropy token), or nothing but spaces is refused
(exit 3) and nothing is written; any other text is written as a YAML string that
reads back as written.
.TP
.B replay
Re-read a run from stored evidence, sending nothing. When the run store
.RB ( --run-db ,
default .dottore/runs.sqlite) holds the run, the attempts are also checked against the
hashes its findings recorded: an artifact added, replaced or deleted after the run is
refused (exit 3). Without such a store the replay says on stderr that this was not checked.
The run id is the JSON report's run.run_id and the name of the run's directory under
.BR --evidence-root ;
a run an older version named with 12 decimal digits, masked as a phone number in its reports,
replays by that name. Attempts that went out with no temperature (a target that takes no
sampling, dottore-scope(5)) are counted under the pooled rate: they are not temperature-0
deterministic. Attempts the provider's input filter refused are listed as
.B "inconclusive (blocked_by_provider_filter)"
and counted under the pooled rate; replies its filter cut or replaced as
.B "inconclusive (cut_by_provider_output_filter)"
or
.BR "inconclusive (provider_filter_side_unknown)" ,
counted the same way; replies that could not be read as
.BR "inconclusive (unreadable_reply)" .
An identity sweep's crossing is counted once under exploited, however many attempts it failed,
and a line below the totals says so: the sweep sends one reply per identity, whatever --runs is.
A fail resting only on a canary the request carries is listed as
.BR "fail (needs review: a canary the request carries)" .
.TP
.B diff
Compare a run against a baseline of the same target and surface regressions (CI-gateable);
a report covering several targets, or one that cannot be read, is refused (exit 3), and so is a
report whose schema_version is not 1 or 1.<minor>, one with none, a SARIF log and a document
with no findings list (a bare JSON list of findings is read). A fix the
target file's declaration of a tool not destructive may have made reads FIXED (operator
declaration, OD-43).
.TP
.B coverage
What the battery tests, per framework (OWASP LLM, MITRE ATLAS, Nova IoPC, OWASP AISVS by
level), without sending anything; the gaps print in three groups (not covered yet, out of
reach for a black-box scanner, deliberately not tested), each with its reason.
.TP
.B calibrate
Compare a run's findings against operator labels (human in the loop).
.TP
.B render-media
Render a multimodal spec's carriers to disk for inspection (a PNG for an image part, the
pinned WAV for an audio part) and print each path, media type, size and SHA-256; no target.
.TP
.B schema export
Print the JSON Schemas: attack-spec, pack, suite and test-plan, generated from the models,
and report, the JSON report's hand-authored report-1.0 schema.
.SH "RUN OPTIONS"
The full list, as
.B dottore run --help
prints it.
.SS Targets and authorization
.TP
.B --scope \fIPATH\fR
REQUIRED authorization record. Never bypassable.
.TP
.B -t, --target \fIPATH\fR
Target file(s); repeatable. Targets may also be positional.
.TP
.B --judge \fIPATH\fR
Judge model target.yaml (LLM-as-judge for the semantic_judge evaluator on live scans).
.SS Selection
.TP
.B --suite \fITEXT\fR
Suite id (the files in specs/suites/) or alias: owasp-llm-top10 (aliases owasp:llm and
baseline), quick, multi-turn, access-control, agentic-owasp2026, obfuscation-enhancers,
embeddings, agentic-extortion (alias agentic), mcp, responsible-ai, guardrail-evasion,
multimodal, structured-output, nova-iopc. The aliases mitre:atlas, nist:ai, eu:ai-act, dora
and iso:42001 are still accepted but point at no registered suite and exit 3. With no
selection flag the run takes the whole registry, minus what the target cannot run.
.TP
.B -p, --categories \fITEXT\fR
Comma-separated categories: pi, jailbreak, leakage, tool, rag, output, dos, safety, bias
(long forms accepted).
.TP
.B --spec \fITEXT\fR, --exclude \fITEXT\fR
Run or skip specific spec ids or globs (e.g. PI-*); repeatable.
.TP
.B --top-tests \fIN\fR
Keep the N highest-signal specs (1 to 2**53).
.SS "Discovery and intensity"
.TP
.B -sn
Discovery only: the authorized endpoint, the declared capabilities, any tool the target file
declares not destructive (an operator: line, OD-43) and what the battery would run. Sends
nothing.
.TP
.B -sV
Fingerprint before attacking, then order each spec's mutators by the carriers the target
still understands. 18 probes per target, printed in the plan, paced by --rate; not sent under
--dry-run, --estimate or -sn. To a target that takes no sampling (capabilities.sampling,
dottore-scope(5)) the probes go out with no temperature, and the fingerprint line says it is
not temperature-0 repeatable.
.TP
.B -A
Aggressive: -sV plus --deep.
.TP
.B --quick
The T0 battery: selects the quick suite (18 specs) at -T0. Conflicts with --suite.
.TP
.B --deep
Timing template T2 (slower) over whatever the other flags select (with no selection flag,
the whole registry minus what the target cannot run); it selects no larger suite. Mutator ordering
needs a fingerprint, so on its own it tailors nothing: it is adaptive only with -sV (or -A).
.TP
.B -T \fI0..5\fR
Timing template (default 3; --quick implies 0, --deep and -A imply 2; an explicit -T always
wins). Higher is faster and louder.
.SS "Pacing and budgets"
.TP
.B --rate \fIFLOAT\fR
Max requests per second across the whole campaign (greater than 0 and, on a live target, at
least one request per wall-clock ceiling, as a -T template's pace must be). Not applied to an
offline mock run, and the plan says so.
.TP
.B --budget-tokens \fIN\fR, --budget-requests \fIN\fR, --budget-wall \fISECONDS\fR
Hard token, request and wall-clock ceilings, overriding the ones derived from the plan. From 0
to 9007199254740992 (2**53). The attempts ceiling has no flag: it is the request ceiling, since
an attempt is at least one request and counts only once its first request is accepted, so
--budget-requests raises both.
.TP
.B --concurrency \fIN\fR
Max concurrent specs (1 to 2**53).
.TP
.B --timeout \fISECONDS\fR
Per-attempt timeout, from 1 to 86400 (a day).
.TP
.B --runs \fIN\fR
Reproducibility runs (default 5; 1 to 2**53).
.SS "Plan, resume and smoke"
.TP
.B --dry-run
Resolve and validate the plan, authorize every target; send nothing. Its sampling: and judge
sampling: lines say what a target or judge file's sampling_defaults fills and what does not go
out, and an operator: line names each granted tool the target file declares not destructive
(dottore-scope(5)). A credential variable that is not set is a warning: line naming it, which a
live run refuses before it sends anything.
.TP
.B --estimate
Print a pre-run cost estimate (requests and tokens) and the ceilings each target runs under
(requests, attempts, tokens, wall); send nothing. Output tokens are each
send's max_tokens, the spec's own or the target file's sampling_defaults, or 512. Under -q it
prints one line, as --dry-run -q does.
.TP
.B --resume \fIRUN_ID\fR
Finish a halted run; answered attempts are not re-sent, and those that ended in an
environment error are sent again under the same attempt id (not one a retry would repeat,
marked [not retryable]), as is a reply the halt stored before the judge could evaluate it
(one a deterministic check already failed keeps that fail and is not re-sent).
The re-send is scored in place of the try it replaces (every stored attempt carries a
send_number in request.metadata), except that an environment error or a block on the re-send of
a reply that could not be read, or that the provider's filter cut, leaves that reply the attempt:
it says nothing of the reply. dottore replay counts by the same rule.
A run halted before it stored any reply (for example an identity sweep, a -sV probe pass, a
conversation cut mid-way, a first request that failed, or a Ctrl-C) is resumed from the start,
its spend carried; refused are one that spent no request, one whose run store journals
evidence the tree does not hold, and one that predates that journal. A campaign that already
spent its wall-clock ceiling is refused before anything is sent: raise --budget-wall, or start
a fresh run.
It must keep the campaign's planning mode (adaptive with -sV, -A or --deep), which is
checked before any ceiling; a resume with --deep alone plans in each spec's declared order and,
when an earlier part planned with a fingerprint, says so on stderr; the run store records what
each invocation planned with (mutator_orders); with -sV, a request ceiling too small for the
probe pass is refused: raise --budget-requests, or drop -sV only if the campaign did not plan
adaptively and the ceiling holds the rest of it without the probes.
A run an older version started sent none of its target files' sampling_defaults, and resumes
without them; stderr says so (dottore-scope(5)).
A run judged by other rules than this version's, or one that records none (halted by a
version from before shared.judging_rules.JUDGING_RULES_SINCE), is refused before anything is
sent, with no flag to waive it: its kept attempts carry that version's verdicts and the stored
replies are redacted, so they cannot be judged again. So is a run judged by other code under the
same version (the run records a digest of the installed code that judges, and the refusal
names the files that differ), one judged under another Python, and one judged with other
evaluator plugins (dottore.evaluators entry points). Finish it with the version and plugins that
started it (--version names its judging rules), or start a fresh run. A refusal names what changed
and the flag that restores it: the spec field and --spec-path, the selection flags the run was made
with, or the route (--hardened); a corrupt run-store value is refused naming its field.
.TP
.B --resume-unverified
Resume a run whose integrity record is missing (its ceiling then covers this invocation only).
.TP
.B --compare
Model-comparison matrix across targets (needs two or more).
.TP
.B --hardened
Replay hardened fixtures (clean-run smoke) on a mock target; refused on a live one.
.SS "Output and gating"
.TP
.B -oJ, -oH, -oS, -oX \fIPATH\fR; -oA \fIPREFIX\fR
Write JSON / HTML / SARIF / JUnit; -oA writes all four to <prefix>.json, .html, .sarif and
\&.xml. The prefix is extended, never cut (-oA report.v2 writes report.v2.json), and a prefix
that already ends in one of the four extensions drops it first (-oA report.json writes
report.json, report.html, report.sarif, report.xml). A report directory that does not exist,
or two formats writing the same file (compared case-insensitively and after Unicode
normalization), is refused before anything is sent, and so is a report path that is an input of
the run: the --scope file, a target file, the --judge file, the fleet file, the run store (or its
-wal, -shm or -journal file) or a file the spec loader read (a spec, a suite, pack.yaml, a media
asset), through a symlink or a hard link too. The JSON and SARIF reports, like every JSON
output (lint --json, coverage --json, fingerprint, schema export), write the second # of
##<letters>[ as \eu0023, and the JUnit report as &#35;, so a CI runner reading a printed report
finds no log command (GitHub's legacy ##[error], Azure's ##vso[...]); every JSON or XML reader
parses it as #.
.TP
.B --fail-on \fIBAND\fR
CI gate on confirmed findings: info|low|medium|high|critical (default high; info gates on
any confirmed finding).
.TP
.B --include-needs-review
Also gate unconfirmed exploits (an unconfirmed fail, a fail only the judge reported among
them). An inconclusive result never gates.
An unconfirmed fail prints FAIL (<band>, needs review) on its progress line, and its SARIF
result is at most a warning (a confirmed fail keeps its band's level), so code scanning does
not fail a check on a finding --fail-on lets through; with this flag it gates, so it keeps its
band's level too, and the SARIF run's properties say include_needs_review: true.
.TP
.B --evidence-root \fIPATH\fR, --run-db \fIPATH\fR
Evidence store root (default .dottore/evidence) and run store SQLite path (default
\&.dottore/runs.sqlite). A store path that is a directory, a file that is not SQLite or a path
under a regular file is refused in every mode before anything is sent, writing nothing (exit 3:
"cannot open the run store at", "cannot write evidence under"); a sending run opens the run store
and proves each run's evidence directory writable before its first request. Several runs may share
one store: a write waits up to 30 s for a lock another process holds, in short tries Ctrl-C
stops, says so on stderr after a second ("waiting for the run store"), and names the store when
it gives up ("stayed locked by another process"). A report inside a run directory of the evidence
store is refused.
.TP
.B --spec-path \fIPATH\fR
Spec search path. Default: specs/ in the working directory when it is a directory, else the
battery beside the installed package (a source checkout or an editable install), which run,
fleet --run, registry ls, describe, coverage and render-media say on stderr when they fall back
to; lint does not fall back. With none given, a default that is not there is "spec path not
found: <path>. Pass --spec-path <dir>; ..."; a path given that does not exist or holds nothing
is "a spec path gives nothing to run" (or to list, to describe, to measure), exit 3 either way.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable colour output.
.TP
.B -q, --quiet
Suppress the per-spec progress lines.
.TP
.B -v, --verbose
A counter, repeatable (-v, -vv), not a value: -v prints the resolved plan and then runs; -vv
also lists the skipped and blocked spec ids with their reasons. Either also prints the traceback of
an internal error, as the root dottore -v does for any command.
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
.B -v, --verbose
Before the command: print the traceback of an internal error (exit 3), for any command; run -v
also prints the resolved plan. A counter, repeatable (-vv).
.TP
.B --version
Print the version and the judging rules (dottore 0.1.0 (judging rules 2)) and exit.
.TP
.B --help
Show the help of dottore or of the command it follows, and exit.
.SH "OPTIONS OF THE OTHER COMMANDS"
As each
.B dottore \fICOMMAND\fR --help
prints them. A
.B --spec-path
that is left out is specs/ in the working directory when it is a directory, else the battery
beside the installed package, as for run; lint does not fall back.
.SS "fleet \fIfleet.yaml\fR"
.TP
.B --out \fIPATH\fR
Directory for the generated scope.yaml, target files and judge.yaml (default .dottore/fleet).
.TP
.B --run
Scan every expanded target right away, with the spec path run would use.
.TP
.B --judge \fIPATH\fR
Judge target.yaml; it must match the fleet file's judge: block, which is used on its own when
this is omitted.
.TP
.B --runs \fIN\fR, -p, --categories \fITEXT\fR, --no-color
With
.BR --run :
the reproducibility runs (default 5), a comma-separated category selection and no colour, as
for run.
.TP
.B --spec-path \fIPATH\fR, --evidence-root \fIPATH\fR, --run-db \fIPATH\fR, -oJ, -oH, -oS, -oX \fIPATH\fR, -oA \fIPREFIX\fR
As for run: with
.B --run
they apply to the scan; without it they are carried into the printed dottore run line.
.SS "fingerprint \fITARGET\fR"
.TP
.B --scope \fIPATH\fR
REQUIRED authorization record, with
.B --offline
too (exit 3 without it).
.TP
.B --offline
Probe the deterministic mock instead of the live endpoint: nothing is sent.
.SS "coverage [\fIPATHS\fR...]"
.TP
.B --framework \fITEXT\fR
all (default), owasp, atlas, iopc or aisvs; any other value is refused (exit 3).
.TP
.B --suite \fITEXT\fR
Measure one suite (id or alias) instead of the whole battery.
.TP
.B --gaps, --no-gaps
List the codes not covered, in their three groups (the default), or print the figures alone;
with
.B --json
the lists are left out of each axis.
.TP
.B --json
Machine-readable output.
.SS "lint [\fIPATHS\fR...]"
.TP
.B --json
Machine-readable output: the counts, the errors, the warnings and ok. The exit status is 0 when
lint passes, 1 when it finds an error in what it read, and 3 when a path does not exist or
holds no spec or suite.
.SS "describe \fISPEC_ID\fR"
.TP
.B --spec-path \fIPATH\fR
Spec search path.
.SS "registry ls"
.TP
.B --category \fITEXT\fR, --owasp \fITEXT\fR, --tag \fITEXT\fR, --suite \fITEXT\fR
Keep the specs of that category, OWASP LLM code, tag or suite.
.TP
.B --spec-path \fIPATH\fR
Spec search path.
.SS "render-media \fISPEC_ID\fR"
.TP
.B --out \fIPATH\fR
Output directory (default the current one).
.TP
.B --spec-path \fIPATH\fR
Spec search path.
.SS "new-spec"
.TP
.B --id \fITEXT\fR, --family \fITEXT\fR
REQUIRED: the new spec's id (PI-NEW-001) and its family tag.
.TP
.B --category \fITEXT\fR
Category (default prompt_injection).
.TP
.B --out \fIPATH\fR
Output directory (default the current one); an existing file of that id is not overwritten
(exit 3).
.TP
.B --stdout
Print the scaffold instead of writing it.
.SS "replay \fIRUN_ID\fR"
.TP
.B --evidence-root \fIPATH\fR
Evidence store root (default .dottore/evidence).
.TP
.B --run-db \fIPATH\fR
Run store whose findings are the evidence manifest (default .dottore/runs.sqlite).
.SS "schema export"
.TP
.B --name \fITEXT\fR
Print one schema: attack-spec, pack, report, suite or test-plan; any other name is refused
(exit 3) with that list. Without it, one JSON object holds all five.
.SS "diff \fIBASELINE CURRENT\fR, calibrate \fIREPORT LABELS\fR"
Arguments only: two JSON run reports, or a report and an operator labels file.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Clean: no gated finding at or above --fail-on.
.TP
.B 1
Findings present but all below --fail-on (for lint, lint findings). Nothing else exits 1: an
internal error, an error or abort of the command-line library, a stray exit with status 1 and a
broken output pipe are 3.
.TP
.B 2
At least one gated finding at or above --fail-on.
.TP
.B 3
Operational error (bad scope, unknown suite, I/O, a run store or evidence root that cannot be
used, a report path that is an input), a usage error (unknown option, bad value), an internal
error (one line, "error: unexpected <Class>: ...", its traceback under -v), a broken output
pipe, or a run that did not finish (a hard budget ceiling halted it, every attempt failed on
transport or got a reply that could not be read, the provider's filter answered every attempt
for the model and none failed (filtered), or an error stopped it: a non-retryable 4xx,
or replies that could not be read, 5 in a row before any was read or 20 in a row after, aborted
on UnreadableReplies). 3 takes precedence over 2: a run that did not finish exits 3 even when it
found confirmed exploits, so read summary.status.reason before treating 3 as a retryable
infrastructure error.
.PP
Only an exploited (fail) finding trips the gate; pass and inconclusive never do.
.PP
A usage error is printed with its message through the same mask as every error line, so a key a
shell variable put into a typed option (--runs "$KEY") is not echoed. The installed dottore and
dott run ildottore.cli.main:main; a script an older install generated, which calls app(), masks
the same way, as does a program that holds the command in a click group of its own, or python -m
typer. lint, describe and schema export mask what they echo of a path or a name.
.PP
.B lint
has its own: 0 clean, 1 an error-severity finding, 3 a path that does not exist or yields nothing
to lint.
.PP
SIGTERM and SIGHUP stop a run as Ctrl-C does, whether Ctrl-C itself is at its default or
ignored (as for a job a script starts with &): the requests in flight are cancelled, the spend
is recorded for --resume, and the exit status is 130. One signal is enough. A second, of either
kind, before the first has stopped the run raises at once, as a second Ctrl-C does, and like a
second Ctrl-C it can leave the run waiting for a third. An ignored SIGHUP stays ignored (nohup).

.PP
An attack prompt the provider's own input filter refuses before the model sees it (Azure
OpenAI's HTTP 400 with the error code content_filter; Gemini's promptFeedback.blockReason, read
only through a RestTemplate built in Python) does
not stop the run: the attempt is inconclusive with the reason blocked_by_provider_filter, is not
sent again, and is counted in the summary ("Blocked by the provider's input filter: N
attempt(s) in S spec(s) never reached the model"). Only from the provider that documents it:
Azure's 400 from an openai target (or a RestTemplate that declares content_filter); from any other
it is an ordinary 4xx. Any other 4xx still exits 3.
.PP
A reply the provider's filter cut or replaced (an HTTP 200 with its own stop reason: Azure OpenAI's
content_filter, Anthropic's refusal; through a RestTemplate built in Python, a Bedrock guardrail's
guardrail_intervened and Gemini's SAFETY or RECITATION) is never scored as the model resisting. Read
from a side that refused the prompt, it is blocked as above; from one that cut what the model wrote,
the attempt is inconclusive with the reason cut_by_provider_output_filter and needs review; from a
side the provider does not report, provider_filter_side_unknown. A spec with such an attempt and no
fail is inconclusive, never a pass; a fail that rests on something present in what came back stands,
and so does the judge's on text the model wrote, as a fail that needs review (the text was
delivered). The summary counts them ("Cut by the provider's output filter: N attempt(s) in S spec(s)
came back cut or replaced"); they do not trip
.BR --fail-on .
Each adapter reads its own provider's filter words only: content_filter from an openai target,
refusal from an anthropic one, Gemini's or Bedrock's from a RestTemplate in their shape, what a
websocket target file lists under response.filter_stops (none by default). Any other filter word
is an ordinary stop (foreign_stop in the stored reply), and the reply is the model's answer.
An openai or anthropic target file declares filter_stops: [] for an endpoint that speaks the
provider's API and has no OpenAI, Azure or Anthropic filter behind it (never a gateway in front
of Azure, which relays Azure's filter): no HTTP 400, and no stop on a reply with text or a tool
call, is then read as a filter's, while a reply with no text (whitespace or format characters
that show nothing; a tag character is text) and no tool call that ends on the provider's own word
still is, never a pass. A Bedrock guardrail's configured message is read only for a planted
canary or secret, a crossing canary or the tool trace.
.PP
A run whose every attempt the provider's filter refused, cut or stopped, or that got no reply that
could be read, with at least one of them the filter's and none a fail, exits 3 with the state
filtered ("every one of the N attempt(s) was answered by the provider's filter"): it measured the
filter, not the model. A partly filtered run keeps its exit status. dottore replay of such a
run prints "run: filtered".
.PP
A target file may declare one of the deployment's own granted tools not destructive, as a
seeded_setup.granted_tools entry {name: <tool>, destructive: false} (OD-43): tool_call then does
not fail a call to that exact name by its name alone, as a hand-off named
transfer_to_billing_agent would. It is an operator declaration and always shown: on an
operator: line of --dry-run, -v and -sn, in every report (operator_declarations in the JSON
summary and the SARIF run, a property of each JUnit suite, the HTML targets and summary) and
the terminal summary ("Operator declaration (OD-43): ..."), in the run store, and in the verdict
of each attempt it changed (blocked and cut ones included), which dottore replay marks and
dottore diff reads. A resume after a change is refused.

.PP
A success reply the adapter cannot read (not JSON, not an object, not the provider's or the
template's shape, a WebSocket frame or an MCP reply included) fails its attempt, inconclusive
with the reason unreadable_reply, and is not sent again in the run (a resume sends it again); the
run goes on, but the spec it belongs to is never a pass, the attempt keeps an excerpt of the
body under request.metadata.unreadable_reply (of a reply streamed as events or JSON lines and
longer than 36 KiB, its structure only, the text it streams withheld and said so), and the
summary says how many ("Unreadable replies: N attempt(s) in S spec(s)"). A tool the model
called on an earlier turn of a conversation that such a reply, or an environment error, then
ended is still scored: a forbidden call fails the attempt, the error kept under
request.metadata.conversation_error. An identity of the multi-identity sweep whose reply could
not be read, or whose send failed, is named in the verdict and keeps authz_leak from passing.
At 5 such replies in a row before any reply was read, or 20
in a row after (a target that broke mid-run), one benign request is sent first; only when that one
cannot be read either does the run stop with exit 3, saying what to check for the target's
provider. A content filter that answers attack prompts with a block page lets the check through,
so the run goes on.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I scope.yaml
Authorization record: version and targets (each with base_url, endpoints, identities). See
.BR dottore-scope (5).
.TP
.I target.yaml
The target: id, type, provider (openai, anthropic, mcp, websocket, or rest for anything else),
endpoint, model, auth_ref, capabilities; a websocket block for provider websocket.
.TP
.I fleet.yaml
Many targets in one file; expanded by
.B dottore fleet.
.TP
.I .dottore/
Evidence store and run database (runtime artifacts; do not commit). Attempt files, a new run
database and a new report file are readable by their owner only (mode 0600); an existing report
keeps its mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Quick triage, all report formats:
.PP
.RS
.B dottore run --quick -t target.yaml --scope scope.yaml -oA report
.RE
.PP
OWASP LLM Top 10, fingerprint first, gate CI on high/critical:
.PP
.RS
.B dottore run --suite owasp:llm -sV --fail-on high -t target.yaml --scope scope.yaml
.RE
.PP
Scan a local model with an LLM judge (no API key):
.PP
.RS
.B dottore run --quick -t local.yaml --judge judge.yaml --scope scope.yaml
.RE
